{
  "headline": "Cadence shows a solid unit economics foundation — 75% gross margin and 2.1x burn multiple — but the model needs significant expansion before it is investor-ready: it lacks forward projections, headcount-driven expense detail, and a fundraising bridge.",
  "investor_talking_points": [
    "Our 75% gross margin is at the top of the SaaS range, and we're holding an acceptable 2.1x burn multiple at $120K/month net burn with $58.5K MRR.",
    "At our 8% monthly growth rate the model projects we will reach profitability before cash runs out — though we're stress-testing that assumption against slower-growth scenarios.",
    "Our static runway is 12.5 months from June 2026, which gives us a defined fundraising window we intend to use to raise our next round with proof-point ARR from this growth trajectory."
  ],
  "lenses": {
    "runway": {
      "callout": "12.5 months of static runway (from $1.5M cash at $120K/mo burn as of June 2026); base projection is default-alive at 8% monthly growth.",
      "highlight": "Slow-growth scenario (reduced assumptions) shows only 13 months of cash — the cushion between base and slow cases is thin, so protecting the revenue growth rate is the key runway lever.",
      "watch_out": "The model's Jan 2025 implied burn ($42.5K/mo) is 2.8x lower than the founder-stated June 2026 burn ($120K/mo), and no headcount or cost plan explains that gap — investors will probe it."
    },
    "unit_economics": {
      "callout": "Gross margin of 75% is strong for seed-stage SaaS; Rule of 40 at -53.3 reflects that growth hasn't yet offset burn, which is expected but needs a clear path forward.",
      "watch_out": "CAC, LTV/CAC ratio, and NRR cannot be computed from the model — these are the first three questions any SaaS investor will ask, and the model currently cannot answer any of them."
    }
  }
}

